<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Viktor_Patsayev> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Viktor Ivanovich Patsayev** (Template:Lang-ru; 19 June 1933 – 29 June 1971) was a [Soviet](:Soviet_Union) [cosmonaut](:Astronaut) who flew on the [Soyuz 11](:Soyuz_11) mission and was part of the third space crew to [die during a space flight](:List_of_spaceflight-related_accidents_and_incidents). On board the space station [Salyut 1](:Salyut_1) he operated the Orion 1 Space Observatory (see [Orion 1 and Orion 2 Space Observatories](:Orion_1_and_Orion_2_Space_Observatories%29); he became the first man to operate a telescope outside the Earth's atmosphere.After a normal [re-entry](:Re-entry), the capsule was opened and the crew was found dead. It was discovered that a valve had opened just prior to leaving orbit that had allowed the capsule's atmosphere to [vent away into space](:Space_exposure), suffocating the crew. One of Patsayev's hands was found to be bruised, and he may have been trying to shut the valve manually at the time he lost consciousness.Patsayev's ashes were interned in the [Kremlin Wall](:Kremlin_Wall_Necropolis) on [Red Square](:Red_Square) in Moscow. He was posthumously awarded the title of [Hero of the Soviet Union](:Hero_of_the_Soviet_Union), the [Order of Lenin](:Order_of_Lenin) and the title of [Pilot-Cosmonaut of the USSR](:Pilot-Cosmonaut_of_the_USSR). The lunar crater *[Patsaev](:Patsaev_%28crater%29)* and the minor planet [1791 Patsayev](:1791_Patsayev) are named for him."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
