<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Soyuz_TMA-19> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Soyuz TMA-19 was a crewed spaceflight to the International Space Station (ISS) and is part of the Soyuz programme. It was launched on 15 June 2010 carrying three members of the Expedition 24 crew to the International Space Station, who remained aboard the station for around six months. Soyuz TMA-19 was the 106th crewed flight of a Soyuz spacecraft, since the first mission which was launched in 1967. The spacecraft remained docked to the space station for the remainder of Expedition 24, and for Expedition 25, to serve as an emergency escape vehicle. It undocked from ISS and landed in Kazakhstan on 26 November 2010. It was the 100th mission to be conducted as part of the International Space Station programme since assembly began in 1998."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Vitaly_Sevastyanov> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Vitaly Ivanovich Sevastyanov** (Template:Lang-ru; 8 July 1935 – 5 April 2010) was a [Soviet](:Soviet_people) [cosmonaut](:Astronaut) and an [engineer](:Engineer) who flew on the [Soyuz 9](:Soyuz_9) and [Soyuz 18](:Soyuz_18) missions.He trained as an engineer at the [Moscow Aviation Institute](:Moscow_Aviation_Institute) and after graduation in 1959, joined [Sergey Korolev](:Sergey_Korolev)'s design bureau, where he worked on the design of the [Vostok spacecraft](:Vostok_spacecraft). He also lectured at the [Cosmonaut Training Centre](:Gagarin_Cosmonaut_Training_Centre), teaching the physics of spaceflight. In 1967 he commenced cosmonaut training himself. Between 15 and 24 September 1972 he Sevastyanov visited [Zagreb](:Zagreb), Yugoslavia.After two successful missions, including a two-month stay on the [Salyut 4](:Salyut_4) [space station](:Space_station), he was pulled from active flight status in 1976. He worked in ground control for the [Salyut 6](:Salyut_6) station before returning to spacecraft design in the 1980s to work on the [Buran](:Shuttle_Buran) project.In 1971, he was the backup Flight Engineer for the ill-fated [Soyuz 11](:Soyuz_11) Mission, which ended in disaster when the craft depressurized above the [Kármán line](:K%C3%A1rm%C3%A1n_line), killing the three man crew.He was president of the [Soviet Chess Federation](:USSR_Chess_Federation) from 1977 to 1986 and from 1988 to 1989.During the 1980s he was the host of a popular television program on space exploration entitled *Man, Earth, Universe*.In 1993, he left the space programme and was elected to the [State Duma](:State_Duma) in 1994.Sevastyanov, along with [Aleksey Leonov](:Alexei_Leonov), [Rusty Schweickart](:Rusty_Schweickart) and [Georgy Grechko](:Georgy_Grechko) established the [Association of Space Explorers](:Association_of_Space_Explorers) in 1984. Membership is open to all people who have flown in [outer space](:Outer_space).He died in Moscow on 5 April 2010, aged 74."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
