<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Waterbury,_Connecticut> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Waterbury** is a city in the U.S. state of [Connecticut](:Connecticut) on the [Naugatuck River](:Naugatuck_River), 33 miles (53 km) southwest of [Hartford](:Hartford%2C_Connecticut) and 77 miles (124 km) northeast of [New York City](:New_York_City). Waterbury is the second-largest city in [New Haven County, Connecticut](:New_Haven_County%2C_Connecticut). According to the 2020 US Census, in 2020 Waterbury had a population of 114,403. As of the [2010 census](:2010_United_States_Census), Waterbury had a population of 110,366, making it the 10th largest city in the [New York Metropolitan Area](:New_York_Metropolitan_Area), 9th largest city in [New England](:New_England) and the 5th largest city in [Connecticut](:Connecticut).Throughout the first half of the 20th century, Waterbury had large industrial interests and was the leading center in the United States for the manufacture of brassware (including castings and finishings), as reflected in the nickname the \"[Brass](:Brass) City\" and the city's motto *Quid Aere Perennius?* (\"What Is More Lasting Than Brass?\"). It was also noted for the manufacture of watches and clocks ([Timex](:Timex_Group_USA%29).The city is alongside [Interstate 84](:Interstate_84_%28Pennsylvania%E2%80%93Massachusetts%29) (Yankee Expressway) and [Route 8](:Connecticut_Route_8) and has a [Metro-North](:Metro-North) [railroad station](:Waterbury_%28Metro-North_station%29) with connections to [Grand Central Terminal](:Grand_Central_Terminal). Waterbury is also home to [Post University](:Post_University) and the regional campuses of the [University of Connecticut](:University_of_Connecticut), [University of Bridgeport](:University_of_Bridgeport), [Western Connecticut State University](:Western_Connecticut_State_University) as well as [Naugatuck Valley Community College](:Naugatuck_Valley_Community_College)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
