<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Deke_Slayton> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Donald Kent** \"**Deke**\" **Slayton** (March 1, 1924 – June 13, 1993) was a [United States Air Force](:United_States_Air_Force) [pilot](:Aviator), [aeronautical engineer](:Aeronautical_engineer), and [test pilot](:Test_pilot) who was selected as one of the original [NASA](:NASA) [Mercury Seven](:Mercury_Seven) [astronauts](:Astronaut). He went on to become NASA's first [Chief of the Astronaut Office](:Chief_of_the_Astronaut_Office) and Director of Flight Crew Operations, responsible for NASA crew assignments.Slayton joined the [U.S. Army Air Forces](:U%2ES%2E_Army_Air_Forces) during [World War II](:World_War_II), and flew in [Europe](:European_theatre_of_World_War_II) and the [Pacific](:Asiatic-Pacific_Theater). He left the Army after World War II, went on to receive a [Bachelor of Science](:Bachelor_of_Science) degree in [aeronautical engineering](:Aerospace_engineering) from [University of Minnesota](:University_of_Minnesota) in 1949, and later joined the [Minnesota Air National Guard](:Minnesota_Air_National_Guard) after working for [Boeing](:Boeing) as an [aeronautical engineer](:Aeronautical_engineer). He joined the [United States Air Force](:United_States_Air_Force), and attended the U.S. [Air Force Test Pilot School](:Air_Force_Test_Pilot_School) in 1955. In 1959, he applied to, and was selected as one of the [Mercury Seven](:Mercury_Seven), NASA's first class of astronauts. Slayton was scheduled to pilot the second U.S. crewed [orbital spaceflight](:Orbital_spaceflight), but was grounded in 1962 by [atrial fibrillation](:Atrial_fibrillation), an irregular heart rhythm. In March 1972, he was medically cleared to fly and was the docking module pilot of the 1975 [Apollo–Soyuz Test Project](:Apollo%E2%80%93Soyuz_Test_Project) (ASTP). Slayton continued to work at NASA until 1982. He also helped develop the [Space Shuttle](:Space_Shuttle).He died from a brain tumor on June 13, 1993, aged 69."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
