<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Wally_Schirra> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Walter Marty Schirra Jr.** (, March 12, 1923 – May 3, 2007) was an American [naval aviator](:United_States_Naval_Aviator), [test pilot](:Test_pilot), and [NASA astronaut](:NASA_astronaut). In 1959, he became one of the [original seven](:Mercury_Seven) astronauts chosen for [Project Mercury](:Project_Mercury), which was the [United States](:United_States)' first effort to put human beings into [space](:Outer_space). On October 3, 1962, he flew the six-orbit, nine-hour, [Mercury-Atlas 8](:Mercury-Atlas_8) mission, in a spacecraft he nicknamed *Sigma 7*. At the time of his mission in *Sigma 7*, Schirra became the fifth American and ninth human to travel into space. In the two-man [Gemini program](:Project_Gemini), he achieved the first [space rendezvous](:Space_rendezvous), [station-keeping](:Orbital_station-keeping) his [Gemini 6A](:Gemini_6A) spacecraft within 1 foot (30 cm) of the sister [Gemini 7](:Gemini_7) spacecraft in December 1965. In October 1968, he commanded [Apollo 7](:Apollo_7), an 11-day [low Earth orbit](:Low_Earth_orbit) shakedown test of the three-man [Apollo Command/Service Module](:Apollo_Command/Service_Module) and the first crewed launch for the [Apollo program](:Apollo_program).Before becoming an astronaut, Schirra graduated with a [Bachelor of Science](:Bachelor_of_Science) degree from the [United States Naval Academy](:United_States_Naval_Academy) in 1945, and served at sea during [World War II](:World_War_II). In 1948, he became a naval aviator, served as a [fighter pilot](:Fighter_pilot) and flew 90 [combat missions](:Aerial_warfare) in the [Korean War](:Korean_War), and then in 1958 he graduated from the [U.S. Naval Test Pilot School](:United_States_Naval_Test_Pilot_School). Schirra retired from the Navy in 1969 with the rank of [captain](:Captain_%28naval%29).He was the first astronaut to go into space three times, and was the only astronaut to have flown in the Mercury, Gemini, and Apollo programs. He was not the only Mercury astronaut, however, to be in all three programs; [Gus Grissom](:Gus_Grissom) did it too. In total, Schirra logged 295 hours and 15 minutes in space. After [Apollo 7](:Apollo_7), he retired as a captain from the U.S. Navy as well as from NASA, subsequently becoming a consultant to [CBS News](:CBS_News) in the network's coverage of following Apollo flights. Schirra joined [Walter Cronkite](:Walter_Cronkite) as co-anchor for all seven of NASA's Moon landing missions."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Latvia> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Latvia (/ˈlɑːtviə/ or /ˈlætviə/; Latvian: Latvija [ˈlatvija]; Latgalian: Latveja; Livonian: Leţmō), officially the Republic of Latvia (Latvian: Latvijas Republika, Latgalian: Latvejas Republika, Livonian: Leţmō Vabāmō), is a country in the Baltic region of Northern Europe. It is one of the Baltic states; and is bordered by Estonia to the north, Lithuania to the south, Russia to the east, Belarus to the southeast, and shares a maritime border with Sweden to the west. Latvia covers an area of 64,589 km2 (24,938 sq mi), with a population of 1.9 million. The country has a temperate seasonal climate. Its capital and largest city is Riga. Latvians belong to the ethno-linguistic group of the Balts; and speak Latvian, one of the only two surviving Baltic languages. Russians are the most promine"@en .
