<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Denver> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Denver** () is a [consolidated city and county](:List_of_municipalities_in_Colorado%23Consolidated_city_and_county), the [capital](:List_of_capitals_in_the_United_States%23State_capital), and [most populous city](:List_of_municipalities_in_Colorado) of the [U.S.](:United_States) [state of Colorado](:Colorado). Its population was 715,522 a 19.22% increase since the [2010 United States census](:2010_United_States_census). It is the [19th-most populous city](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) in the United States and the fifth most populous state capital. It is the principal city of the [Denver–Aurora–Lakewood, CO Metropolitan Statistical Area](:Denver%E2%80%93Aurora%E2%80%93Lakewood%2C_CO_Metropolitan_Statistical_Area) and the first city of the [Front Range Urban Corridor](:Front_Range_Urban_Corridor).Denver is located in the [Western United States](:Western_United_States), in the [South Platte River Valley](:South_Platte_River) on the western edge of the [High Plains](:High_Plains_%28United_States%29) just east of the [Front Range](:Front_Range) of the [Rocky Mountains](:Rocky_Mountains). Its downtown district is immediately east of the confluence of [Cherry Creek](:Cherry_Creek_%28Colorado%29) and the [South Platte River](:South_Platte_River), approximately 12 mi (19 km) east of the [foothills](:Foothills) of the Rocky Mountains. It is named after [James W. Denver](:James_W%2E_Denver), a governor of the [Kansas Territory](:Kansas_Territory). It is [nicknamed](:List_of_city_nicknames_in_the_United_States) the *Mile High City* because its official elevation is exactly one mile (5280 feet or 1609.344 meters) above sea level. The [105th meridian west of Greenwich](:105th_meridian_west), the longitudinal reference for the [Mountain Time Zone](:Mountain_Time_Zone), passes directly through [Denver Union Station](:Denver_Union_Station).Denver is ranked as a [Beta world city](:Alpha_world_city) by the [Globalization and World Cities Research Network](:Globalization_and_World_Cities_Research_Network). The 10-county [Denver-Aurora-Lakewood, CO Metropolitan Statistical Area](:Denver-Aurora-Lakewood%2C_CO_Metropolitan_Statistical_Area) had a population of 2,963,821 at the [2020 United States census](:2020_United_States_census), making it the [19th most populous U.S. metropolitan statistical area](:List_of_metropolitan_statistical_areas). The 12-county [Denver-Aurora, CO Combined Statistical Area](:Denver-Aurora%2C_CO_Combined_Statistical_Area) had a population of 3,623,560 at the 2020 United States census, making it the [17th most populous U.S. primary statistical area](:List_of_primary_statistical_areas). Denver is the most populous city of the 18-county [Front Range Urban Corridor](:Front_Range_Urban_Corridor), an oblong urban region stretching across two states with a population of 5,055,344 at the 2020 United States Census. Its metropolitan area is the most populous metropolitan area within a 560-mile (900 km) radius and the second most populous city in the [Mountain West](:Mountain_States) after [Phoenix, Arizona](:Phoenix%2C_Arizona). In 2016, it was named the best place to live in the United States by *[U.S. News & World Report](:U%2ES%2E_News_%26_World_Report)*."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
