<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Detroit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Detroit** ( [*dih-TROYT*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key), locally also  [*DEE-troyt*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key); Template:Lang-fr, lit. 'strait') is the [largest city](:List_of_municipalities_in_Michigan) in the [U.S. state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Michigan](:Michigan). It is also the largest U.S. city on the [United States–Canada border](:Canada%E2%80%93United_States_border), and the [seat of government](:County_seat) of [Wayne County](:Wayne_County%2C_Michigan). The City of Detroit had a population of 639,111 at the [2020 census](:2020_United_States_census), making it the [27th-most populous city](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) in the United States. The [metropolitan area](:Metropolitan_area), known as [Metro Detroit](:Metro_Detroit), is home to 4.3 million people, making it the second-largest in the [Midwest](:Midwestern_United_States) after the [Chicago metropolitan area](:Chicago_metropolitan_area), and the 14th-largest in the United States. Regarded as a major cultural center, Detroit is known for its contributions to [music](:Music_of_Detroit), art, [architecture](:Architecture_of_metropolitan_Detroit) and design, in addition to its historical automotive background. *[Time](:Time_%28magazine%29)* named Detroit as one of the fifty World's Greatest Places of 2022 to explore.Detroit is a major port on the [Detroit River](:Detroit_River), one of the four major [straits](:Strait) that connect the [Great Lakes](:Great_Lakes) system to the [Saint Lawrence Seaway](:Saint_Lawrence_Seaway). The City of Detroit anchors the second-largest regional economy in the [Midwest](:Midwest), behind [Chicago](:Chicago) and ahead of [Minneapolis–Saint Paul](:Minneapolis%E2%80%93Saint_Paul), and the [14th-largest](:List_of_U%2ES%2E_metropolitan_areas_by_GDP) in the United States. Detroit is best known as the center of the [U.S. automobile industry](:Automotive_industry_in_the_United_States), and the \"[Big Three](:Big_Three_%28automobile_manufacturers%29)\" auto manufacturers [General Motors](:General_Motors), [Ford](:Ford_Motor_Company), and [Stellantis North America](:Chrysler) (Chrysler) are all headquartered in Metro Detroit. As of 2007[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Detroit&action=edit), the Detroit metropolitan area is the number one exporting region among 310 defined metropolitan areas in the United States. The [Detroit Metropolitan Airport](:Detroit_Metropolitan_Airport) is among the most important [hub airports](:Airline_hub) in the United States. Detroit and its neighboring Canadian city [Windsor](:Windsor%2C_Ontario) are connected through [a highway tunnel](:Detroit-Windsor_Tunnel), [railway tunnel](:Michigan_Central_Railway_Tunnel), and the [Ambassador Bridge](:Ambassador_Bridge), which is the second-busiest international crossing in North America, after [San Diego–Tijuana](:San_Diego%E2%80%93Tijuana). Both cities will soon be connected by a new bridge currently under construction, the [Gordie Howe International Bridge](:Gordie_Howe_International_Bridge), which will provide a complete freeway-to-freeway link. The new bridge is expected to be open by 2024.In 1701, [Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac](:Antoine_de_la_Mothe_Cadillac) and [Alphonse de Tonty](:Alphonse_de_Tonty) founded [Fort Pontchartrain du Détroit](:Fort_Pontchartrain_du_D%C3%A9troit), the future city of Detroit. During the late nineteenth and early twentieth century, it became an important industrial hub at the center of the [Great Lakes region](:Great_Lakes_region). The city's population became the fourth-largest in the nation in 1920, after only [New York City](:New_York_City), Chicago and [Philadelphia](:Philadelphia), with the expansion of the [auto industry](:Automotive_industry_in_the_United_States) in the early 20th century. As Detroit's industrialization took off, the [Detroit River](:Detroit_International_Riverfront) became the busiest commercial hub in the world. The strait carried over 65 million tons of shipping commerce through Detroit to locations all over the world each year; the freight throughput was more than three times that of New York and about four times that of [London](:London). By the 1940s, the city's population remained the fourth-largest in the country. However, due to industrial restructuring, the loss of jobs in the auto industry, and rapid [suburbanization](:Suburbanization), among other reasons, Detroit entered a state of [urban decay](:Decline_of_Detroit) and lost considerable population from the late 20th century to the present. Since reaching a peak of 1.85 million at the [1950 census](:1950_United_States_Census), Detroit's population has declined by more than 65 percent. In 2013, Detroit [became the largest U.S. city to file for bankruptcy](:Detroit_bankruptcy), which it successfully exited in December 2014, when the city government regained control of Detroit's finances.[Detroit's diverse culture](:Culture_of_Detroit) has had both local and international influence, particularly in [music](:Music_of_Detroit), with the city giving rise to the genres of [Motown](:Motown) and [techno](:Techno_music), and playing an important role in the development of [jazz](:Detroit_jazz), [hip-hop](:Music_of_Detroit%23Hip_hop), [rock](:Music_of_Detroit%23rock_and_roll), and [punk](:Music_of_Detroit%23punk). The rapid growth of Detroit in its boom years resulted in a globally unique stock of [architectural monuments](:Architecture_of_metropolitan_Detroit) and [historic places](:National_Register_of_Historic_Places_listings_in_Detroit%2C_Michigan). Since the 2000s, [conservation](:Conservation-restoration_of_cultural_heritage) efforts have managed to save many architectural pieces and achieved several large-scale [revitalizations](:Planning_and_development_in_Detroit), including the restoration of several historic [theatres](:Performing_arts_in_Detroit) and entertainment venues, [high-rise](:List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Detroit) renovations, new sports stadiums, and a riverfront revitalization project. More recently, the population of [Downtown Detroit](:Downtown_Detroit), [Midtown Detroit](:Midtown_Detroit), and various other neighborhoods have increased. An [increasingly popular tourist destination](:Tourism_in_metropolitan_Detroit), Detroit receives 16 million visitors per year. In 2015, Detroit was named a [\"City of Design\"](:Creative_Cities_Network) by [UNESCO](:UNESCO), the first U.S. city to receive that designation."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
