<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Lancaster,_California> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Lancaster**  is a [charter city](:Charter_city) in northern [Los Angeles County](:Los_Angeles_County%2C_California), in the [Antelope Valley](:Antelope_Valley) of the western [Mojave Desert](:Mojave_Desert) in [Southern California](:Southern_California). As of the [2020 census](:2020_United_States_Census), the population was 173,516, making Lancaster the [153rd largest city in the United States](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) and the [30th largest in California](:List_of_largest_cities_in_California_by_population). Lancaster is part of a [twin city](:Twin_cities) complex with its southern neighbor [Palmdale](:Palmdale%2C_California), and together they are the principal cities within the Antelope Valley region.Lancaster is located approximately 70 miles (110 km) north (via [I-5](:Interstate_5_in_California) and [SR 14](:California_State_Route_14%29) of downtown [Los Angeles](:Los_Angeles), and is near the [Kern County](:Kern_County%2C_California) line. It is separated from the [Los Angeles Basin](:Los_Angeles_Basin) by the [San Gabriel Mountains](:San_Gabriel_Mountains) to the south, and from [Bakersfield](:Bakersfield%2C_California) and the [San Joaquin Valley](:San_Joaquin_Valley) by the [Tehachapi Mountains](:Tehachapi_Mountains) to the north. The population of Lancaster has grown from 37,000 at the time of its incorporation in 1977 to over 157,000 as of 2019."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/University_of_Kansas> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **University of Kansas** (**KU**) is a [public](:Public_university) [research university](:Research_university) with its main campus in [Lawrence, Kansas](:Lawrence%2C_Kansas), United States, and several satellite campuses, research and educational centers, medical centers, and classes across the state of Kansas. Two branch campuses are in the [Kansas City metropolitan area](:Kansas_City_metropolitan_area) on the Kansas side: the university's [medical school](:University_of_Kansas_School_of_Medicine) and [hospital](:University_of_Kansas_Hospital) in [Kansas City, Kansas](:Kansas_City%2C_Kansas), the [Edwards Campus](:University_of_Kansas_Edwards_Campus) in [Overland Park](:Overland_Park%2C_Kansas). There are also educational and research sites in [Garden City](:Garden_City%2C_Kansas), [Hays](:Hays%2C_Kansas), [Leavenworth](:Leavenworth%2C_Kansas), [Parsons](:Parsons%2C_Kansas), and Topeka, an agricultural education center in rural north Douglas County, and branches of the medical school in [Salina](:Salina%2C_Kansas) and [Wichita](:Wichita%2C_Kansas). The university is a member of the [Association of American Universities](:Association_of_American_Universities) and is [classified](:Carnegie_Classification_of_Institutions_of_Higher_Education) among \"R1: Doctoral Universities – Very high research activity\".Founded March 21, 1865, the university was opened in 1866, under a charter granted by the [Kansas State Legislature](:Kansas_State_Legislature) in 1864 and legislation passed in 1863 under the [State Constitution](:Constitution_of_Kansas), which was adopted two years after the 1861 admission of the former [Kansas Territory](:Kansas_Territory) as the 34th state into the Union.Enrollment at the Lawrence and Edwards campuses was 23,958 students in fall 2021; an additional 3,727 students were enrolled at the KU Medical Center (KUMC) for an enrollment of 27,685 students across the three campuses. The university overall (including KUMC) employed 4,776 faculty members (faculty, faculty administrators, graduate student employees and librarians) in fall 2021.Kansas's athletic teams compete in NCAA Division I sports as the Jayhawks, as members of the [Big 12 Conference](:Big_12_Conference). They field 16 varsity sports, as well as club-level sports for ice hockey, rugby, and men's volleyball."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
