<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Charleston,_South_Carolina> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Charleston** is the largest city in the [U.S. state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [South Carolina](:South_Carolina), the [county seat](:County_seat) of [Charleston County](:Charleston_County%2C_South_Carolina), and the principal city in the [Charleston–North Charleston metropolitan area](:Charleston_metropolitan_area%2C_South_Carolina). The city lies just south of the geographical midpoint of South Carolina's coastline on [Charleston Harbor](:Charleston_Harbor), an inlet of the Atlantic Ocean formed by the confluence of the [Ashley](:Ashley_River_%28South_Carolina%29), [Cooper](:Cooper_River_%28South_Carolina%29), and [Wando](:Wando_River) rivers. Charleston had a population of 150,277 as of the [2020 U.S. Census](:2020_United_States_census). The 2020 population of the Charleston metropolitan area, comprising [Berkeley](:Berkeley_County%2C_South_Carolina), [Charleston](:Charleston_County%2C_South_Carolina), and [Dorchester](:Dorchester_County%2C_South_Carolina) counties, was 799,636 residents, the third-largest in the state and the 74th-largest metropolitan statistical area in the United States.Charleston was founded in 1670 as Charles Town, honoring [King Charles II](:Charles_II_of_England), at Albemarle Point on the west bank of the [Ashley River](:Ashley_River_%28South_Carolina%29) (now [Charles Towne Landing](:Charles_Towne_Landing%29) but relocated in 1680 to its present site, which became the fifth-largest city in [North America](:North_America) within ten years. It remained [unincorporated](:Municipal_corporation) throughout the colonial period; its government was handled directly by a colonial legislature and a governor sent by [Parliament](:Parliament_of_Great_Britain). Election districts were organized according to [Anglican](:Anglicanism) parishes, and some social services were managed by Anglican [wardens](:Churchwarden) and [vestries](:Vestry). Charleston adopted its present spelling with its incorporation as a [city](:City_%28United_States%29) in 1783. Population growth in the interior of South Carolina influenced the removal of the state government to [Columbia](:Columbia%2C_South_Carolina) in 1788, but Charleston remained among the ten largest cities in the United States through the [1840 census](:1840_U%2ES%2E_Census).Charleston's significance in American history is tied to its role as a major slave trading port. Charleston slave traders like [Joseph Wragg](:Joseph_Wragg) were the first to break through the monopoly of the [Royal African Company](:Royal_African_Company) and pioneered the large-scale slave trade of the 18th century; almost one half of slaves imported to the United States arrived in Charleston. In 2018, the city formally apologized for its role in the American Slave trade after CNN noted that slavery \"riddles the history\" of Charleston."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
