<http://dbpedia.org/resource/University_of_Notre_Dame> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **University of Notre Dame du Lac**, known simply as **Notre Dame** ( [*NOH-tər-DAYM*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key%29) or **ND**, is a private [Catholic](:Catholic_university) [research university](:Research_university) in [Notre Dame, Indiana](:Notre_Dame%2C_Indiana), outside the city of [South Bend](:South_Bend%2C_Indiana). French priest [Edward Sorin](:Edward_Sorin) founded the school in 1842. [The main campus](:Campus_of_the_University_of_Notre_Dame) covers 1,261 acres (510 [ha](:Hectare%29) in a suburban setting and contains landmarks such as the [Golden Dome](:Main_Building_%28University_of_Notre_Dame%29), the [*Word of Life* mural](:Word_of_Life_mural) (commonly known as *Touchdown Jesus*), [Notre Dame Stadium](:Notre_Dame_Stadium), and the [Basilica](:Basilica_of_the_Sacred_Heart_%28Notre_Dame%29). Originally for men, although some women earned degrees in 1918, the University began formally accepting numerous undergraduate female students in 1972.Notre Dame has been recognized as one of the top universities in the United States. The university is organized into seven schools and colleges. Notre Dame's graduate program includes more than 50 master, doctoral and professional degrees offered by the six schools, including the [Notre Dame Law School](:Notre_Dame_Law_School) and an [MD–PhD](:MD%E2%80%93PhD) program offered in combination with the [Indiana University School of Medicine](:Indiana_University_School_of_Medicine). The [School of Architecture](:Notre_Dame_School_of_Architecture) is known for teaching [New Classical Architecture](:New_Classical_Architecture) and for awarding the annual [Driehaus Architecture Prize](:Driehaus_Architecture_Prize). The university offers more than 50 year-long study programs abroad and over 15 summer programs.Notre Dame maintains a system of libraries, cultural venues, artistic and scientific museums, including the [Hesburgh Library](:Hesburgh_Library) and the [Snite Museum of Art](:Snite_Museum_of_Art). Most of the university's 8,000 undergraduates live on campus in one of [33 residence halls](:List_of_residence_halls_at_the_University_of_Notre_Dame), each with its own traditions, legacies, events, and [intramural sports](:Intramural_sports) teams. The university's approximately 134,000 alumni constitute one of the strongest college alumni networks in the U.S.The university's athletic teams are members of the [NCAA Division I](:NCAA_Division_I) and are known collectively as the [Fighting Irish](:Notre_Dame_Fighting_Irish). Notre Dame is noted for its [football team](:Notre_Dame_Fighting_Irish_football), which contributed to its rise to prominence on the national stage in the early 20th century; the team, an [Independent](:NCAA_Division_I_FBS_independent_schools) with no conference affiliation, has accumulated 11 consensus national championships, seven [Heisman Trophy](:Heisman_Trophy) winners, 62 members of the [College Football Hall of Fame](:College_Football_Hall_of_Fame), and 13 of the [Pro Football Hall of Fame](:Pro_Football_Hall_of_Fame). Notre Dame teams in other sports, chiefly in the [Atlantic Coast Conference](:Atlantic_Coast_Conference), have accumulated 17 national championships. The [\"Victory March\"](:Notre_Dame_Victory_March) is one of the most famous and recognizable collegiate fight songs.Notre Dame's profile grew in the early 20th century, aided by the success of its football team under coach [Knute Rockne](:Knute_Rockne). Major improvements to the university occurred during [Theodore Hesburgh](:Theodore_Hesburgh)'s administration between 1952 and 1987. His administration increased the university's resources, academic programs, and its reputation. Since then, the university has seen steady growth, and under the leadership of the next two presidents, [Edward Malloy](:Edward_Malloy) and [John I. Jenkins](:John_I%2E_Jenkins), many infrastructure and research expansions have been completed. Notre Dame's growth has continued in the 21st century. At the end of the [fiscal year](:Fiscal_year) 2021, its [endowment](:Financial_endowment) was valued at $20.3 billion, one of the largest among U.S. universities."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/California_Institute_of_Technology> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/wikiPageRevisionID> "1123291414"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Auburn_University> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Auburn University (AU or Auburn) is a public land-grant research university in Auburn, Alabama. With more than 24,600 undergraduate students and a total enrollment of more than 30,000 with 1,330 faculty members, Auburn is the second largest university in Alabama. It is one of the state's two public flagship universities. The university is classified among \"R1: Doctoral Universities – Very High Research Activity\" and its alumni include 5 Rhodes Scholars and 5 Truman Scholars."@en .
