<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Buzz_Aldrin> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Buzz Aldrin** (; born **Edwin Eugene Aldrin Jr.**; January 20, 1930) is an American former [astronaut](:Astronaut), [engineer](:Engineer) and [fighter pilot](:Fighter_pilot). He made three [spacewalks](:Extravehicular_activity) as pilot of the 1966 [Gemini 12](:Gemini_12) mission. As the [Lunar Module *Eagle*](:Lunar_Module_Eagle) pilot on the 1969 [Apollo 11](:Apollo_11) mission, he and mission commander [Neil Armstrong](:Neil_Armstrong) were the first two people to land on the [Moon](:Moon).Born in [Glen Ridge, New Jersey](:Glen_Ridge%2C_New_Jersey), Aldrin graduated third in the class of 1951 from the [United States Military Academy](:United_States_Military_Academy) at West Point, with a degree in [mechanical engineering](:Mechanical_engineering). He was commissioned into the [United States Air Force](:United_States_Air_Force), and served as a jet fighter pilot during the [Korean War](:Korean_War). He flew 66 [combat missions](:Aerial_warfare) and shot down two [MiG-15](:MiG-15) aircraft.After earning a [Doctor of Science](:Doctor_of_Science) degree in [astronautics](:Astronautics) from the [Massachusetts Institute of Technology](:Massachusetts_Institute_of_Technology), Aldrin was selected as a member of [NASA](:NASA)'s [Astronaut Group 3](:Astronaut_Group_3), making him the first astronaut with a doctoral degree. His doctoral thesis was *Line-of-Sight Guidance Techniques for Manned Orbital Rendezvous*, earning him the nickname \"Dr. Rendezvous\" from fellow astronauts. His first space flight was in 1966 on Gemini 12 during which he spent over five hours on [extravehicular activity](:Extravehicular_activity). Three years later, Aldrin set foot on the Moon at 03:15:16 on July 21, 1969 ([UTC](:Coordinated_Universal_Time%29), nineteen minutes after Armstrong first touched the surface, while [command module pilot](:Command_module_pilot) [Michael Collins](:Michael_Collins_%28astronaut%29) remained in lunar orbit. A [Presbyterian](:Presbyterian_Church_%28U%2ES%2EA%2E%29) [elder](:Elder_%28Presbyterian%29), Aldrin became the first person to hold a religious ceremony on the Moon when he privately took [communion](:Eucharist). Apollo 11 effectively proved US victory in the [Space Race](:Space_Race), by fulfilling a national goal proposed in 1961 by President [John F. Kennedy](:John_F%2E_Kennedy) \"of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely to the Earth\" before the end of the decade.Leaving NASA in 1971, Aldrin became Commandant of the [U.S. Air Force Test Pilot School](:U%2ES%2E_Air_Force_Test_Pilot_School). He retired from the Air Force in 1972, after 21 years of service. His autobiographies *Return to Earth* (1973), and *[Magnificent Desolation](:Magnificent_Desolation_%28book%29)* (2009), recount his struggles with [clinical depression](:Clinical_depression) and [alcoholism](:Alcoholism) in the years after leaving NASA. Aldrin continues to advocate for [space exploration](:Space_exploration), particularly a [human mission to Mars](:Human_mission_to_Mars), and developed the [Aldrin cycler](:Aldrin_cycler), a special spacecraft [trajectory](:Trajectory) that makes travel to Mars more efficient in terms of time and propellant. He has been accorded numerous honors, including the [Presidential Medal of Freedom](:Presidential_Medal_of_Freedom) in 1969."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
