<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Joe_Engle> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Joe Henry Engle** (born August 26, 1932) is an American [pilot](:Aviator), [aeronautical engineer](:Aeronautical_engineer) and former [NASA](:NASA) [astronaut](:Astronaut). He was the commander of two [Space Shuttle](:Space_Shuttle) missions including [STS-2](:STS-2) in 1981, the program's second orbital flight. He also flew three flights in the Shuttle program's 1977 [Approach and Landing Tests](:Approach_and_Landing_Tests). Engle is one of twelve pilots who flew the [North American X-15](:North_American_X-15), an experimental [spaceplane](:Spaceplane) jointly operated by the Air Force and NASA.As an X-15 pilot, Engle made three flights above 50 miles, thus qualifying for [astronaut wings](:Astronaut_wings) under the American convention for the boundary of space. In 1966 he was selected for NASA's [fifth Astronaut Group](:NASA_Astronaut_Group_5), joining the [Apollo program](:Apollo_program). He was the backup [Lunar Module](:Lunar_Module) Pilot (LMP) for [Apollo 14](:Apollo_14) and originally scheduled as LMP for [Apollo 17](:Apollo_17). However, cancellation of later flights prompted NASA to select geologist-astronaut [Harrison Schmitt](:Harrison_Schmitt) as LMP, displacing Engle.Engle is an experienced spaceplane operator and the last living X-15 pilot."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
